{"type":"text","timestamp":"2026-10-19T20:47:16.692Z","data":{"text":"`compute_seal` is written and its own verify passes, but the proposal's verify fails. `cargo test -p osq-core --test seal_compute` passes all 11 tests, one for each table row plus the UTF-16 path order case.\n\nThe failure is in `crates/core/tests/no_io.rs`. It pins core's `[dependencies]` to exactly `serde` and `serde_json`, so the `sha2.workspace = true` line the task requires makes it fail. That file is outside this task's scope and the task doesn't allow changing existing tests, so I left it alone. It needs a task that includes the file. The fix is to expect `sha2` and take each key's name before the first `.`.\n\nBecause `sh scripts/verify-rust.sh` stops at that test, its mutants step never ran. The clippy check did run, and I fixed the one lint error it raised.\n\nThe two entry files named in the failure from attempt 2 do exist in the worktree (`src/core/spec/hasher.ts` and `crates/core/tests/edges.rs`). That failure looks like a path-resolution problem in the harness, not missing files.\n\nThe result file is at `openspec/changes/223-rust-approval-seal/.run/results/1.md`. It records the blocker under `## Blocked`.\n\nThe implementation is in `crates/core/src/seal.rs`, with the normalisation in the new `crates/core/src/seal/normalize.rs`."}}
